        "width": 4096,
        "height": 3072,
        "description": "This photo was taken in the Philippine National Museum\nContext:\n\"THE PARIAN DISTRICT OF CEBU CITY\"\nAt the beginning of Cebu as the first Spanish settlement in the Philippines, the colonizers wanted to keep different nationalities separate. To implement this, Miguel Lopez de Legazpi divided Cebu into two, the poblacion de naturales which became San Nicolas, for the natives, and the poblacion de europeos, which became the ciudad or city of Cebu, for the Europeans. In 1590, the Chinese settled to the north of Cebu, becoming a trading hub called the Parian district, from a Chinese term meaning \"trade\" or \"barter. \n\nThe homes and stores that made up the Parian district represent distinct architectural style and are lasting examples of the unique Houses such as the Casa Gorordo, the Yap-San Diego House, and the Jesuit House to the built heritage and historic urban context of the Parian, and Cebu as a whole.",
